ned and this could  potentially also affect international  markets like the european market  all right so turkey has ordered that  crunch and supplies for three days iran  meanwhile saying that it needs 10 days  to solve its problem what is turkey  going to do in the meantime  well the government has to try to solve  this because the fear here in the  country right now is apart from the  obvious economic and financial damage  that the blackouts and the gas supply  restrictions could also affect  households although the government has  pledged that wouldn't be the case now  officials from the state-run turkish  pipeline company botash have been sent  on an emergency mission to tehran to  try to resolve this stoppage but you  have to know that these disruptions  really come at a very delicate time for  turkey demand for energy has skyrockets  especially skyrocketed especially now  that it's a winter but the turkish  currency the lira has weakened very  badly against the dollar and the euro so  imports of energy have become very very  expensive both for the industry and for  households  you know gas supplies are in high demand  right now around the world that's  probably only going to increase in the  years ahead with a transition for many  countries is this prompting any kind of  a rethink for ankara  well this is what experts and government  critics have been demanding in recent  days highlighting that turkey is nearly  fully dependent on imports gas imports  from azerbaijan russia and iran so these  experts are basically saying that turkey  has to diversify energy suppliers  increase energy efficiency improve  storage or develop alternative energy  resources so what they are saying is the  government has to be prepared much  better for future out outages  all right yulia hahn in istanbul thank  you very much
 

